# Day 54

**40km / 8 hours**

*Ngaporo campsite to The Flying Fox*

Managed to get organised and set off a bit earlier for the day. There promised to be some decent rapids to traverse which made a sense of trepidation abundant. I packed gear so more of it was inside the waterproof barrels where I had been more cavalier on the previous days. Early on there were some rapids where you just had to navigate correctly going into them to avoid hitting rocks or logs but these were comfortable. Further on there was a rapid which is commonly called the '50/50' since that's the chance of the canoe capsizing when going through it. However we just bounced through it and even though it was a little shaky there wasn't any danger and felt comfortable enough. After a while we came to the jetty at Pipiriki to take a break. 

![The river as it has been all the time](./river.avif)

It was a gentle walk up the hill where there was network reception, toilets and somewhere to get a nice burger to eat for a morning tea. Civilisation! It took a while to get that meal but was worth it to relax and sort out some plans. After that we coasted down further. Most people canoeing the river just stop at Pipiriki since it's convenient for the canoe operator so this is a bit of an exclusive section. This also means some exclusive rapids to pass.

![Another photo of the river, it's hard to take good photos in the canoe though since the phone isn't waterproof](./river-2.avif)

We came to a rapid that looked even more gnarly than the prior ones since the only path was on the left side next to some trees with branches around so our group actually just stopped on the rocks and carried the canoes over instead. Kind of a cowardly move and most likely would've been okay to ride the rapid but that's okay. Other rapids we went down pretty comfortably and they made progress pretty quick so we got to the private campsite in good time although it does mean a pretty big day tomorrow. 

There were a ridiculous number of goats gallivanting around on the banks of the river. I do love seeing the ducks and ducklings and there were even some geese and goslings around the river. Didn't see any water creatures under the river since it's so murky. Have seen an excessive number of insects though, many of them intimately after they attach themselves to me in some way which isn't pleasant.

One weird thing about canoeing for me is that often when I sit or lie down afterwards my head still feels like it's going up and down as if I were still in the canoe. I do enjoy the canoeing but I think I'll be happier when back on my feet and walking properly again. It's less mentally taxing and more relaxing. My feet are also feeling a bit different after not wearing my hiking boots for a week or so so that's interesting. Will see how we go tomorrow where we should get off the river.